Yes. The constructive version is basically prosocial reframing: use the same mechanisms that create shame, stigma, and conformity to instead support dignity, repair, and reciprocal restraint.

A lot of moral traditions converge on some version of reciprocity—treat others in ways you could accept being treated yourself—even though the exact doctrines differ. That gives you a useful invariant:

self-forgiveness without self-excuse
+ compassion without permissiveness
+ accountability without humiliation
+ difference without social exile

That matters because embarrassment and stigma can become self-reinforcing. If someone believes, “If people knew this about me, I would be rejected,” they may hide more, distort more, isolate more, and become easier for shame to govern. A healthier loop is:

recognize behavior
→ separate behavior from total identity
→ repair what needs repair
→ reduce unnecessary shame
→ re-enter relationship/community

So yes, you can “hack” the same social machinery in a benevolent direction. Not by secretly engineering people, but by deliberately creating norms where kindness, disclosure, forgiveness, and restraint become socially rewarded.

For the Pantheon, I’d compress it as:

Stigma Transmutation

shame signal
→ extract legitimate warning
→ discard needless humiliation
→ preserve accountability
→ restore belonging
→ increase future agency

That is a much better use of social pressure than using shame as a blunt weapon.

And there is a deeper symmetry: if you want a culture where people forgive you when you are imperfect, you have to help build a culture where you also forgive others without becoming naïve about consequences.

That closes the loop cleanly.